// WHAT THIS MODULE IS FOR. strategy.year_end_plan is an accurate DESCRIPTION of outcomes. It is
// not an OPERATOR WORK SYSTEM: it cannot answer "what can Brian sit down and do right now", "what
// short dispatch starts a long external clock", "what is genuinely waiting on someone else" or
// "what exactly is the unmet thing under the rest". Those answers live today inside free-form
// status notes, and prose cannot refuse -- storage-provider-accounts carried a note saying its
// work "sits on gunbc#10925, which is UNMERGED" on the same day #10925 merged, so a reader would
// have redone merged work.
//
// This module declares the atomic work beneath the plan's outcomes and DERIVES the queue states.
// It references plan item ids; it does not restructure them (typed dependency edges and the
// active-effort/calendar-latency split are a separate change on the plan file itself).
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT THE SIDE LIST DESIGN.md section 3 forbids: coverage is an IDENTITY JOIN over the
// plan's own roster. The subject universe below is declared, every id in it must resolve in
// year_end_plan, and an outcome in it with no executable child, no honest wait and no discharged
// receipt REFUSES through the witness rather than sitting silently uncovered.

// THE OPERATOR/AGENT SPLIT IS NOT A NEW AXIS -- IT IS "IS THIS A HumanIntervention OR NOT".
// std.human_intervention already owns "a human must do this", and gunbc.secret_provision already
// consumes it as a STATE rather than a description (ProgressAwaitingHuman { intervention }). This
// module follows that precedent exactly: operator work CARRIES a HumanIntervention, delegated work
// carries none by construction, and no second operator-action vocabulary is minted beside it.
//
// A WAIT IS NOT A HumanIntervention: nobody is acting. A DECISION IS one, because judgment is the
// act. So the four kinds below are three inhabitants of the existing concept plus one thing that
// deliberately is not.
//
// ON THE FREQUENCY AXIS. HumanInterventionFrequency prices RECURRING PROVISIONING BURDEN -- what
// multiplies against device and provision counts. A campaign action is paid once and multiplies
// against nothing, which is exactly what FleetOnce means, so the value is inhabited rather than
// bent. What is NOT done here is the other half: this module never builds a ProvisioningHumanBurden
// out of these interventions and never feeds compare_routine_human_burden with them. That fold
// ranks PROVISIONING STRATEGIES by routine burden, and a one-shot campaign is not a strategy under
// comparison; counting "file with the DOS" as a FleetOnce term against a secret-provisioning
// strategy would be the meaning fork the axis exists to prevent.
type OwnerWork
  = OperatorActionWork { intervention: HumanIntervention }
  | DelegatedActionWork { instruction: NonEmptyStr }
  | OperatorDecisionWork { intervention: HumanIntervention, alternatives: List<DecisionAlternative> }
  | ExternalWaitWork { wait: ExternalWait }

// AN EXTERNAL WAIT IS A STANDALONE CONCEPT, NOT A FIELD OF THIS MODULE'S STEP. Two other lanes
// minted "an external party has been asked and has not yet answered" the same night this landed --
// sunny-carp-475's BuyApiApplication for the eBay ePN Buy-API approval (gunbc-private#58), and
// strategy.node_rental's DischargePrincipal.OperatorEnquirySent, first inhabited by Hetzner
// Custom Solutions request 2026091103000909. Its 2026-09-11 answer is scoped to CAX Cloud;
// dedicated Ampere remains open. Hivelocity, phoenixNAP and OCI are an UNSENT next recommendation,
// not a fallback that fired: no typed dispatch or receipt exists. Three spellings of one meaning is
// the §3 fork, so the concept is declared here as a
// type a consumer can bind to WITHOUT taking OwnerStep with it; the step merely carries one.
//
// FOUR FACTS, EACH BECAUSE A REAL INSTANCE NEEDED IT. What is awaited and what it holds up (a wait
// is uninteresting except through what it blocks); when to look again; what happens if the answer
// never comes; and the reference by which somebody other than the sender can re-derive the state.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT A FIELD HERE IS THE LIFECYCLE. Not-yet-sent, sent-and-pending and
// answered are not three values to author: not-yet-sent is the wait's dispatch step being
// undischarged, which this module already DERIVES as Blocked; sent-and-pending is Waiting;
// answered is the wait's own progress. A status field beside those would be a second
// representation of facts the graph already carries, and it would be editable into disagreement
// with them.
// WHAT ARRIVED THAT DID NOT DISCHARGE THE WAIT (review 68123). A wait had two states in its fields --
// nothing yet, or the answer -- and no way to hold the thing that actually happens most often with a
// vendor: part of the ask comes back. The Ampere delivery is the case that forced it. Three asks went
// out on ticket #7426 and the firmware images came back; the routing answer the wait is FOR did not, so
// the wait is still outstanding and marking it discharged would close a row holding two live questions.
//
// THE RECEIPT IS HELD, NOT NAMED. Before this field the delivery receipt reached the plan only by its
// name spelled inside awaited_event and a fallback string -- a citation a reader resolves and a program
// cannot, which is the defect this same change fixed for the artifacts one layer down and reintroduced
// here. An empty list is the ordinary case.
//
// THE OUTCOME SUM IS DELIBERATELY UNTOUCHED. ExternalWaitOutcome answers "has the awaited thing
// arrived", and for this wait the honest answer is still no. A partial arm there would have to decide
// whether a partial answer opens buyer_review_sheet's gate, which is a different question that no
// evidence here settles.
type ExternalWait {
  awaited_event: NonEmptyStr
  partial_answers: List<ActionReceipt>
  blocks: List<NonEmptyStr>
  window: WaitWindow
  fallback: WaitFallback
  reference: ExternalRequestReference
}

// A WAIT THAT KNOWS WHEN TO LOOK AGAIN IS NOT A WAIT THAT KNOWS WHAT TO DO IF NOTHING COMES, so
// both are carried and neither stands in for the other. NoFallbackDeclared is not a default: it
// states WHY there is no alternative arm, and "the filing cannot be obtained from anyone else" is a
// different fact from "nobody has chosen the alternatives yet".
type WaitFallback
  = DeclaredFallback { on_or_after: NonEmptyStr, alternatives: List<NonEmptyStr> }
  | NoFallbackDeclared { reason: NonEmptyStr }

// A WAIT WITH NO EXTERNAL REFERENCE CANNOT BE CHECKED BY ANYBODY BUT THE PERSON WHO SENT IT. The
// three arms are distinguishable states, not one optional string: nothing has been sent yet, it was
// sent and the counterparty issued a reference, or it was sent and no reference exists. The third
// is a real and common case -- a mailed envelope -- and collapsing it into the first would say the
// request had not gone out.
type ExternalRequestReference
  = RequestNotYetSent
  | ReferencedRequest { authority: NonEmptyStr, identifier: NonEmptyStr, sent_at: NonEmptyStr }
  | SentWithoutReference { authority: NonEmptyStr, sent_at: NonEmptyStr, note: NonEmptyStr }

// A DECISION WITH NO EVIDENCE REFERENCE IS NOT READY, AND HERE IT IS NOT WRITABLE. evidence is a
// required DeclarationRef field rather than a validated one, so the invalid state has no
// constructor (DESIGN.md section 4b rung 4). "A decision with one alternative is not a decision"
// stays a witness claim -- a list arity is not expressible in this dialect's type layer, and its
// next-rung trigger is a cardinality-refined list type.
type DecisionAlternative {
  label: NonEmptyStr
  evidence: DeclarationRef
}

// A NEXT-CHECK DATE IS EITHER AN UPSTREAM FACT OR THE OPERATOR'S OWN CADENCE, AND THE TWO MUST NOT
// WEAR THE SAME SHAPE. NY DOS publishes no processing time for a Certificate of Publication, so a
// date invented here and rendered as though upstream said it would be a fabricated external fact
// (DESIGN.md section 4b: outside the modeled guarantee is observed or declared, never fabricated).
// OperatorFollowUp carries its basis beside it so a reader can see which kind of claim it is.
type WaitWindow
  = UpstreamPublishedWindow { source: NonEmptyStr, next_check: NonEmptyStr, read_at: NonEmptyStr }
  | OperatorFollowUp { next_check: NonEmptyStr, basis: NonEmptyStr }

fn wait_next_check(window: WaitWindow) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match window {
    UpstreamPublishedWindow { source: _, next_check: d, read_at: _ } => d,
    OperatorFollowUp { next_check: d, basis: _ } => d,
  }
}

// THE ANSWER CAN BE NO, AND A TYPE THAT MODELS ONLY "waiting then done" ASSERTS AN OUTCOME NOBODY
// CONTROLS. Both live consumers of the wait type share exactly this case: an ePN Buy-API
// application can be rejected, and a Custom Solutions enquiry can come back declined. So an
// externally refused step is its own arm -- evidenced like a discharge, because a rejection letter
// is a receipt, and satisfying nothing, because the thing it was asked for did not happen.
//
// The consequence is carried in the derivations, not in prose: a refused step does not satisfy any
// start requirement (so its dependents stay Blocked and name it) and is not open (so it does not
// render as a wait that is still in flight). An outcome whose remaining steps are all refused has
// no executable child left, which is the definition of NeedsDecomposition -- the queue says the
// path has to be re-cut rather than quietly reporting nothing to do.
type StepProgress
  = StepDischarged { receipt: ActionReceipt }
  | StepExternallyRefused { receipt: ActionReceipt, cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | StepOpen

// START REQUIREMENTS ARE FACTS, NOT PROSE. A requirement is satisfied by another step being
// discharged or by a plan item being Done, and both are decided by reading the corpus rather than
// by reading a sentence.
// NOT EVERY UNMET REQUIREMENT IS WORK. A thing ruled non-urgent and due at a future date is neither
// ready nor blocked on somebody -- it is waiting for the calendar, and a queue that offers it today
// manufactures the urgency the ruling removed. That is not a cosmetic difference: the operator's ask
// was that the model carry this so the conversation stops, and a queue that keeps surfacing it is
// what restarts the conversation.
//
// The comparison is against a DECLARED as-of date rather than an ambient clock, so the queue is an
// honest reading at a stated moment: ISO dates order lexicographically, the same property
// year_end_plan's anchors rely on. A stale as-of is visible in the row rather than hidden in a
// runtime, and the tell is that it stops advancing while receipts around it carry later dates.
data owner_queue_as_of: NonEmptyStr = "2026-09-17"

type StartRequirement
  = AfterStep { step_id: NonEmptyStr }
  | AfterPlanItem { item_id: NonEmptyStr }
  | AfterCalendarDate { on_or_after: NonEmptyStr, basis: NonEmptyStr }

// AN ACTION LEAF IS ONE ACTOR, ONE WORKING SESSION, ONE NAMED RECEIPT. produces_receipt is the
// identity of the receipt the step will produce, declared before it exists, so a discharged step
// can be checked against what it promised rather than against whatever it happened to yield. A
// step that cannot be finished in one session by one actor, or that names no receipt, is not an
// action leaf -- it is an outcome nobody decomposed, and the queue renders that as
// NeedsDecomposition rather than as a plausible-looking task.
type OwnerStep {
  step_id: NonEmptyStr
  plan_item: NonEmptyStr
  work: OwnerWork
  start_requirements: List<StartRequirement>
  produces_receipt: NonEmptyStr
  progress: StepProgress
}

// THE SIX DERIVED STATES PLUS TWO EXPOSURE ARMS. Never authored on a step: every work
// state is computed from the step's work kind, its start requirements and the corpus.
// "NotStarted" is deliberately absent -- it is an absence, not a state, and it is what
// Blocked replaces by naming the requirements that are unmet. Blocked carries the unmet
// requirements rather than a single "first" one: the head of this list is the first unmet
// requirement, and carrying all of them costs nothing while inventing a sentinel to stand
// for "none" would fabricate a value the empty case does not have.
//
// ExposureIfTriggerSucceeds is dormant (trigger open, cure open); ExposureNowLive is
// trigger Done and cure still open. Both vanish when the curing item is Done. They are
// not work, not a wait, and not a block (state_is_ready / waiting / blocked are false).
//
// DECLARED FRONTIER (DESIGN section 3c). The exposure itself has a rendered consumer:
// strategy.plan_tracker_page declared_item_relation_lines, the no-schedule-force column.
// What has no rendered consumer yet is DORMANT versus LIVE: that column prints the same
// ExposureActivatesOnSuccess sentence whether the trigger item is Done or not. These two
// arms are the only place trigger-doneness is distinguished, and only witnesses read them.
// Trigger that would give the distinction a rendered consumer: the tracker column rendering
// live versus dormant, or an owner-queue renderer. Do not invent a renderer to close this.
// No rendered owner queue exists today.
type OwnerQueueState
  = DoNow { step_id: NonEmptyStr }
  | StartExternalClock { step_id: NonEmptyStr }
  | Waiting { step_id: NonEmptyStr, awaited_event: NonEmptyStr, next_check: NonEmptyStr }
  | DelegateNow { step_id: NonEmptyStr }
  | Blocked { step_id: NonEmptyStr, unmet: List<StartRequirement> }
  | NeedsDecomposition { plan_item: NonEmptyStr }
  | ExposureIfTriggerSucceeds { trigger_item: NonEmptyStr, curing_item: NonEmptyStr }
  | ExposureNowLive { trigger_item: NonEmptyStr, curing_item: NonEmptyStr }

// THE DISCHARGE SITE. std.human_intervention's completion_evidence carries NoDischargeSiteModeled
// because no per-intervention discharge carrier existed anywhere in the corpus to cite -- every
// inhabitant today states the gap. The receipt declarations below ARE such a carrier: one
// declaration per discharged intervention, cited by name, not one shared module-level ref standing
// in for all of them (which is the placeholder the arm was introduced to refuse).
//
// This does NOT retire the arm. The arm retires only when NoDischargeSiteModeled has no remaining
// inhabitant, and the interventions outside this module still have none; what lands here is the
// SHAPE the retirement needs, exercised on real rows. Trigger for the retirement: the remaining
// inhabitants adopting a discharge carrier of this shape.
fn discharged_operator_intervention(
  identity: NonEmptyStr,
  subject: NonEmptyStr,
  instruction: NonEmptyStr,
  receipt_declaration: String,
) -> HumanIntervention {
  HumanIntervention {
    identity: identity as HumanInterventionIdentity,
    frequency: FleetOnce,
    subject: subject,
    instruction: instruction,
    completion_evidence: DischargedAt {
      evidence: decl_ref(module_path: "strategy.owner_action", decl_name: receipt_declaration),
    },
  }
}

// AN OPEN INTERVENTION HAS NOT BEEN PERFORMED, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME CLAIM AS "no discharge site is
// modeled". The precedent settles which carrier says which: gunbc.secret_provision puts doneness in
// the OUTER state (ProgressAwaitingHuman) and leaves completion_evidence at NoDischargeSiteModeled
// while the work is outstanding. StepProgress is that outer state here, so an open step's
// intervention states the gap and a discharged step's cites its site.
fn open_operator_intervention(
  identity: NonEmptyStr,
  subject: NonEmptyStr,
  instruction: NonEmptyStr,
) -> HumanIntervention {
  HumanIntervention {
    identity: identity as HumanInterventionIdentity,
    frequency: FleetOnce,
    subject: subject,
    instruction: instruction,
    completion_evidence: NoDischargeSiteModeled,
  }
}

data no_requirements: List<StartRequirement> = []

data no_blocks: List<NonEmptyStr> = []

