// ANSWERED ON THE TICKET, NOT IN A NEW THREAD (2026-09-15). Ampere support (Abhi) replied to the
// operator's 2026-09-12 message with four triage questions - platform and quantity, purchase route,
// project, current issues - and the operator's answer carried the three asks this step was holding.
// The firmware ask was split deliberately: Ampere is asked to NAME THE ROUTE (the ODM, if board BMC
// and BIOS images are Foxconn's to distribute) rather than to hand over third-party board firmware,
// because the second invites a refusal and the first costs them nothing. The Table 3 ask cites the
// PUBLIC OCP Mt. Jade specification and the attribution the specification itself makes to the
// processor, so it is answerable without an NDA and is the ask most likely to open the relationship.
data receipt_ampere_firmware_asks_sent: ActionReceipt = ActionReceipt {
  receipt_id: "firmware-asks-sent",
  evidence: ExternalRequestSent {
    recipient: "Ampere Support Portal, ticket #7426 (support agent Abhi), from briansrls@gunb.ai: 21 Foxconn Mt. Collins systems / 42 Q80-30 processors, bought on the secondary market with no OEM entitlement; the US East Coast Arm capacity project; and the three asks - an authoritative source or ODM route for BMC, BIOS and SCP images; the channel-membership and rank-organization constraints behind OCP Mt. Jade section 7.4 Table 3; and a route to whoever evaluates NDA requests, or a referral to the correct ODM or distributor.",
    sent_at: "2026-09-15",
  },
}

data step_firmware_asks_dispatch: OwnerStep = OwnerStep {
  step_id: "firmware-asks-dispatch",
  plan_item: "mt-collins-firmware-serviceability",
  work: OperatorActionWork {
    intervention: discharged_operator_intervention(
      identity: "firmware-asks-dispatch",
      subject: "Ampere ticket #7426, three unsent asks",
      instruction: "Send the three prepared asks the ticket did not carry: an authoritative source OR the ODM route for firmware images on owned Mt. Collins systems, the Table 3 channel/rank question, and a named commercial contact or NDA route.",
      receipt_declaration: "receipt_ampere_firmware_asks_sent",
    ),
  },
  start_requirements: no_requirements,
  produces_receipt: "firmware-asks-sent",
  progress: StepDischarged { receipt: receipt_ampere_firmware_asks_sent },
}

// THE IMAGE HALF ARRIVED, AND IT IS A RECEIPT RATHER THAN A CLOSED STEP (2026-09-18/19). Bobby
// delivered three files on ticket #7426 through a temporary upload; the operator downloaded them,
// acknowledged so the upload could be removed, and re-hosted them on his own Drive. What they are, what
// their digests are and what the vendor manifests verify is carried by
// strategy.vendor_correspondence mt_collins_firmware_delivery, which HOLDS its three DeliveredArtifact
// rows, rather than by this sentence -- because the fact a later flash depends on is WHICH bytes, and a
// digest is the only durable answer to that.
//
// THE FIRMWARE HALF IS STRUCK FROM awaited_event RATHER THAN LEFT STANDING, and the receipt is not
// named there either: partial_answers holds it, and a wait whose identity field also spelled the
// receipt would make that prose a second authority for the edge beside it. An awaited event still
// naming something that arrived is the stale-queue defect this module corrected once for
// operator_ratification_pass. Recorded here because this is a remark about the modelling, and a data
// row is not where a remark about the modelling belongs (review 68161).
//
// WHY THE STEP BELOW STAYS OPEN. It waits on an ANSWER, and what arrived answers one of three asks. The
// images are a one-time hand-over, not an entitlement: no authoritative source or ODM route was named,
// the Table 3 question is untouched, and no commercial contact or NDA route exists. Marking the wait
// discharged because the most tangible ask was satisfied is the shape where a partial answer closes a
// row that was holding two other questions, and they then belong to nobody.
data receipt_ampere_firmware_delivered: ActionReceipt = ActionReceipt {
  receipt_id: "firmware-images-delivered",
  evidence: ObservedFact {
    subject: "Ampere Support Portal ticket #7426, Mt. Collins firmware and platform delivery",
    observation: "Three artifacts delivered and independently verified: the AptioV BIOS 2.10c / 0ACOD014 and Altra SCP 2.10 bundle, the MegaRAC BMC 0.45.3 and Foxconn management bundle (BMC and Redfish user guides, System FW Upgrade Guide), and the Mt. Collins Getting Started Guide v1.05 of 2026-07-20. Digests, fetch routes and the executed manifest verification are carried per artifact by strategy.vendor_correspondence mt_collins_firmware_delivery, whose delivered field HOLDS the three artifact rows rather than naming them in prose -- so a reader reaches them by following the reply, not by resolving spellings out of this sentence. THE OBSERVATION IS OF THE FILES, NOT OF A FLASH: nothing here establishes that any image has been applied to any unit, and step_boot_minimum_reset_receipt remains unexecuted with its artifact input now available.",
    observed_at: "2026-09-19",
  },
}

data step_firmware_asks_wait: OwnerStep = OwnerStep {
  step_id: "firmware-asks-wait",
  plan_item: "mt-collins-firmware-serviceability",
  work: ExternalWaitWork {
    wait: ExternalWait {
      partial_answers: [receipt_ampere_firmware_delivered],
      awaited_event: "Ampere response on the memory population question (OCP Mt. Jade section 7.4 Table 3), on a commercial contact or NDA route, and on an authoritative source or ODM route for FUTURE images -- a third thing, not folded into the other two, because one hand-over establishes no route to the next.",
      blocks: ["mt-collins-firmware-serviceability"],
      window: OperatorFollowUp {
        next_check: "2026-09-22",
        basis: "one week after the 2026-09-15 answer on ticket #7426; Ampere support publishes no response time. Unlike the colo forms and the Ubicloud note, this thread HAS an identifier, so a follow-up quotes the ticket rather than starting again.",
      },
      fallback: DeclaredFallback {
        on_or_after: "2026-10-15",
        alternatives: ["record FirmwareImagesUnavailable FOR FUTURE LEVELS and admit production under the bounded replacement-only policy - narrowed on 2026-09-19 because the images we hold today arrived, and a fallback still calling them unavailable would understate the position on the date it fires", "source future images through the ODM or the reseller channel"],
      },
      reference: ReferencedRequest {
        authority: "Ampere Support Portal",
        identifier: "ticket #7426",
        sent_at: "2026-09-15",
      },
    },
  },
  start_requirements: [AfterStep { step_id: "firmware-asks-dispatch" }],
  produces_receipt: "firmware-asks-answered",
  progress: StepOpen,
}

// FABRIC SWITCH 100G: THE FS SUPPORT THREAD. FS support (Nia) replied on 2026-09-18 asking for the
// CRS812 breakout procedure -- forced speed, auto-negotiation off -- and whether the ConnectX-7 was
// actually up while the switch reported link-ok. Both were answered by one operator-approved
// retest on the srv5 leg (public gunbc docs/plans/fabric-switch-actuation-log.md, "FS support
// escalation"): FS's exact configuration left the host in Polling with no carrier while the
// switch reported link-ok/100Gbps. The redacted evidence packet went back the same day. The
// thread is email with no ticket identifier read into the corpus, so the wait is
// SentWithoutReference and is replaced by ReferencedRequest the moment one is cited.
data receipt_fs_fabric_evidence_sent: ActionReceipt = ActionReceipt {
  receipt_id: "fs-fabric-evidence-sent",
  evidence: ExternalRequestSent {
    recipient: "FS technical support (Nia), email thread on the CRS812 / QDD-400G-4QPC015 100G breakout",
    sent_at: "2026-09-18",
  },
}

data step_fabric_fs_evidence_dispatch: OwnerStep = OwnerStep {
  step_id: "fabric-fs-evidence-dispatch",
  plan_item: "fabric-switch-100g",
  work: OperatorActionWork {
    intervention: discharged_operator_intervention(
      identity: "fabric-fs-evidence-dispatch",
      subject: "FS support thread, CRS812 100G breakout",
      instruction: "Answer FS's two asks: confirm the forced-speed / autoneg-off configuration was applied, state whether the ConnectX-7 was operationally up (it was not: Polling, no carrier, 100% ping loss while the switch read link-ok/100Gbps), and attach the redacted port, module and configuration captures from both ends.",
      receipt_declaration: "receipt_fs_fabric_evidence_sent",
    ),
  },
  start_requirements: no_requirements,
  produces_receipt: "fs-fabric-evidence-sent",
  progress: StepDischarged { receipt: receipt_fs_fabric_evidence_sent },
}

// FS REPLIED ON 2026-09-21 AND ANSWERED NONE OF THE THREE AWAITED OUTCOMES. What the reply says,
// what it settles and what it leaves open are carried by strategy.vendor_correspondence
// fs_fabric_100g_retest_request, which this receipt HOLDS by reference rather than restating -- the
// same discipline the firmware delivery row above was corrected into. The receipt exists so the wait
// can record that something arrived without recording that anything was answered: FS asks for one
// more test, so the thread's blocking party has flipped from FS to us, and the step below that runs
// their test is what the wait now depends on.
data receipt_fs_fabric_reply_received: ActionReceipt = ActionReceipt {
  receipt_id: "fs-fabric-reply-received",
  evidence: CorpusDeclaration {
    declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "strategy.vendor_correspondence", decl_name: "fs_fabric_100g_retest_request"),
  },
}

// THE FEC=AUTO CELL WAS RUN ON 2026-09-21 AND PRODUCED A CANDIDATE CAUSE THAT IS NOT ABOUT THE
// CABLE. The run itself, its readings, the restore verification and the procedural defect in the
// fec-mode probe are carried by the public actuation log (docs/plans/fabric-switch-actuation-log.md,
// "FS FEC=Auto retest"), which is where an operator reproducing it reads the operation shapes. What
// this receipt carries is the OUTCOME, because that is what the wait below consumes.
//
// THE MEASURED HALF AND THE INFERRED HALF ARE SEPARATED DELIBERATELY (DESIGN section 4d). Measured:
// the ConnectX-7 accepts RS-FEC(544,514) ONLY at 100G_2X and RS(528,514) at 50G_2X; the CRS812
// accepts exactly fec74, fec91, auto and off, refusing fec119, fec134, rs-fec544 and rs544; and with
// fec-mode=auto the port resolved to fec91. Inferred: that the disjointness of those two sets at
// 100G_2X is the cause of the failure. The inference is a bet typed as one and carried to FS as a
// question, not a finding announced as settled -- and it is a CONSEQUENTIAL bet, because if it holds
// then neither a recode of the QSFP-DD end nor a cable substitution can produce a 100G link here,
// which is the two remedies the thread has been organised around since 2026-09-17.
data receipt_fs_fabric_fec_auto_result: ActionReceipt = ActionReceipt {
  receipt_id: "fs-fabric-fec-auto-result",
  evidence: ObservedFact {
    subject: "CRS812 qsfp56-dd-1-1 to ConnectX-7 spark-a3ee (srv5), FS's requested FEC=Auto configuration",
    observation: "NO 100G LINK. With the switch at auto-negotiation=no, speed=100G-baseCR2, fec-mode=auto and the ConnectX-7 on Auto for both speed and FEC, the two ends disagreed and the leg kept running at 50G: switch link-ok at rate 100Gbps with the resolved mode reported as fec91, host Active at 50G over 2 lanes with RS(528,514), autoneg ON, carrier 1 and 0 percent ping loss, identical at +10s and +30s. This does not contradict the 2026-09-18 Polling result, it explains it: the host kept auto-negotiation ON this time, so against an autoneg-disabled switch port it stayed where it already was rather than dropping. Restored to 50G-baseCR2 / fec91 and verified from both ends. THE FEC DATA FS ASKED FOR IS WHAT CARRIED THE FINDING: the ConnectX-7 reports FEC capability 0x80 (RS-FEC(544,514)) at 100G_2X and 0x7 (No-FEC, Firecode, RS(528,514)) at 50G_2X, while the CRS812 fec-mode admits only fec74, fec91, auto and off and resolves auto to fec91 -- so at 100GBASE-CR2 the switch presents RS(528,514) where the NIC accepts only RS(544,514), which is the 802.3cd FEC for that mode. Measured: both capability sets and the resolved mode. Inferred and put to FS as a question: that their disjointness is the cause.",
    observed_at: "2026-09-21",
  },
}

data step_fabric_fs_fec_auto_retest: OwnerStep = OwnerStep {
  step_id: "fabric-fs-fec-auto-retest",
  plan_item: "fabric-switch-100g",
  work: OperatorActionWork {
    intervention: discharged_operator_intervention(
      identity: "fabric-fs-fec-auto-retest",
      subject: "FS support thread, CRS812 100G breakout, FEC=Auto cell",
      instruction: "Run FS's requested test ONCE, on the srv5 leg (qsfp56-dd-1-1) only, under the 2026-09-18 procedure: both credentials preloaded before the change, restore to the known-good 50G state armed to run on exit, both ends read at +10s and +30s. Switch: auto-negotiation=no, speed=100G-baseCR2, fec-mode=auto, then bounce. Host: ConnectX-7 returned to Auto for BOTH speed and FEC (mstlink advertising the full speed set, FEC auto), then bounce the netdev. Capture what FS asked for and not merely a verdict: the full FEC configuration AND status from both ends -- switch fec-mode, negotiated/active FEC and the port and module readings; host mstlink FEC configuration, active FEC, ethtool --show-fec and the link state -- plus a ping across the link, at both sample times. Restore and VERIFY the restore from both ends before closing. THE ONLY NEW VARIABLE IS FEC: the switch-side configuration in FS's screenshot is the one already executed on 2026-09-18 and already reported to them with captures, so the reply must say that plainly rather than letting a second run of the same configuration read as new evidence. Say also that this cell is ASYMMETRIC -- a forced, autoneg-off switch port facing an autoneg-on host, the combination under which the host previously read 'Autoneg, No partner detected' -- so a failure here is at least as much a reading of FS's procedure as of the cable. And answer their second question: no other 400G QSFP-DD port is held TODAY (the CRS812 is the only QSFP-DD device in the fleet; every other fabric end is a QSFP56 ConnectX-7), so no cross-test on other equipment is available now; the operator expects a second QSFP-DD device in a few months, which is too far out to serve this thread and is stated to FS as such rather than offered as a plan. DO NOT expand the test to the other seven legs. THE RANKING OF THE SUBSTITUTION CONTROL CHANGED WHEN THIS TEST RAN and the change is recorded here because this instruction is what the run was executed against: the control is still the experiment that isolates cable and coding, but it now sits BELOW the RS-544 question the FEC data raised, because a switch that cannot present RS-FEC(544,514) at 100G-baseCR2 makes a substitution unable to succeed either, and buying a cable to learn that is the expensive way to ask FS a question.",
      receipt_declaration: "receipt_fs_fabric_fec_auto_result",
    ),
  },
  start_requirements: [AfterStep { step_id: "fabric-fs-evidence-dispatch" }],
  produces_receipt: "fs-fabric-fec-auto-result",
  progress: StepDischarged { receipt: receipt_fs_fabric_fec_auto_result },
}

data step_fabric_fs_reply_wait: OwnerStep = OwnerStep {
  step_id: "fabric-fs-reply-wait",
  plan_item: "fabric-switch-100g",
  work: ExternalWaitWork {
    wait: ExternalWait {
      partial_answers: [receipt_fs_fabric_reply_received],
      awaited_event: "FS ENGINEERING ON ONE QUESTION, NARROWED 2026-09-21 BY THE FEC=AUTO RESULT: can the CRS812 present RS-FEC(544,514) on a qsfp56-dd breakout sub-port at 100G-baseCR2 -- under a fec-mode value we have not found, a different port mode, or a firmware level above RouterOS 7.20.8? The thread's two organising remedies are downstream of that answer rather than beside it: if the switch cannot present RS(544,514), then neither a recode of the QSFP-DD end nor a replacement cable produces a 100G link on this pairing, and both become the wrong purchase. The earlier three-outcome phrasing -- configuration error, recode, or replacement cable -- is superseded rather than dropped: the 2026-09-21 reply answered none of them, and this question is what would make any of them decidable.",
      blocks: ["fabric-switch-100g"],
      window: OperatorFollowUp {
        next_check: "2026-09-28",
        basis: "FS publishes no engineering response time. The 2026-09-23 check declared after the 2026-09-18 packet is SPENT: FS replied on 2026-09-21, so the clock restarts from the FEC=Auto result rather than from the packet, three business days after it is sent. Replaced the moment FS states a response time or a case identifier.",
      },
      fallback: DeclaredFallback {
        on_or_after: "2026-10-02",
        alternatives: ["run the substitution control with a known-good dual-compatibility cable (FS SKU 145681 / 101806) in the same ports", "run the same cable against a known-good 100GBASE-CR2 endpoint pair"],
      },
      reference: SentWithoutReference {
        authority: "FS technical support",
        sent_at: "2026-09-18",
        note: "email thread; no FS case or ticket number has been read into the corpus.",
      },
    },
  },
  start_requirements: [AfterStep { step_id: "fabric-fs-fec-auto-retest" }],
  produces_receipt: "fs-fabric-reply",
  progress: StepOpen,
}

