  progress: StepDischarged { receipt: receipt_m2_data_map_authored },
}

// THE OPERATOR INPUT THIS MAP IS WAITING ON -- AND DELIBERATELY NOT ITS RATIFICATION. An earlier
// version of this step told the operator to flip each ProposalStanding to RatifiedByOperator while
// counsel questions stayed flagged, and review 66245 refused it against the module's own gate:
//
//   category_settlement_is_coherent(c) = if standing_is_ratified(c.standing)
//                                        then count(category_open_questions(c)) == 0
//
// category_open_questions concatenates THREE sources, not one -- the standing's own question, the
// per-residency retention question, and the per-basis lawful-basis question, where
// CounselReviewRequired returns its question and the other two arms return empty -- CounselOpinionOnRecord
// because counsel answered, and, since operator_legal_conclusion_ruling, ConcludedInHouse because WE did.
// A ratified row must still carry no open question from ANY source; what changed is that ten of the
// thirteen basis claims no longer put one there. Directing a blanket flip today would still make
// map_settlement_is_coherent false, on the rows that remain.
//
// WHAT THE TWO-RATIFIER MODEL ACTUALLY MEANS, since misreading it is what produced the refused
// version: counsel and the operator being two ratifiers with two types does NOT license two
// independent flips. It means the operator's flip is GATED on counsel's answer. The operator's work
// is upstream of ratification, not a half of it.
//
// AND THAT GATE IS NO LONGER MAP-WIDE (operator_legal_conclusion_ruling, strategy.data_map, 2026-09-18).
// The paragraph above is still exactly right about the rows that carry CounselReviewRequired; what
// changed is that it no longer describes every row. The reason it used to was a modelling error rather
// than a policy: CounselStanding had two arms, so a basis we had concluded ourselves had no way to say
// so and the three basis constructors hard-coded the counsel arm onto every one of the thirteen basis
// claims the nine categories carry. Rows whose error we can correct
// ourselves, or with a customer we can reach, are now ConcludedInHouse and ask nothing.
//
// WHAT IS STILL GATED, AND IT IS NOT NOTHING: the sanctions and abuse bases, and the tax and accounting
// legal-obligation basis on account and billing identity -- the placements that land on a third party's
// rights or in front of a supervisory authority -- plus the retention PERIODS bound to
// counsel_retention_period_review, which are statutory numbers and were never lawful-basis questions.
// So the ratification pass is now partial rather than impossible, and the rows it cannot reach are named
// by their placement rather than by a flat policy.
//
// So this step collects what only the operator can supply and leaves every row
// ProposedAwaitingRatification. Ratification is a later event with its own preconditions, and it is
// deliberately not modelled as work anyone can be told to do today.
//
// THE STEP STAYS OPEN AND ITS INSTRUCTION NARROWED, 2026-09-14. The operator answered the retention
// questions the same day this step landed, and an instruction still asking for answers he has given is
// the stale-queue defect strategy.data_map corrected for operator_ratification_pass -- an act still in
// the queue after it was performed. It is NOT discharged, which is the other half of the correction:
// three figures remain his (the aggressive default for logs, the artifact fetchability period, the
// abuse backoff tiers) alongside the in-scope and row-content confirmation, so closing the step would
// be the opposite error. What changed is that the instruction now names those three rather than
// pointing at retention_open_question as a whole, because that fold has since been re-homed and now
// surfaces questions belonging to a fabric eviction policy and to counsel, which the operator cannot
// answer and should not be sent at.
data step_m2_data_map_operator_inputs: OwnerStep = OwnerStep {
  step_id: "m2-data-map-operator-inputs",
  plan_item: "m2-data-map",
  work: OperatorActionWork {
    intervention: open_operator_intervention(
      identity: "m2-data-map-operator-inputs",
      subject: "strategy.data_map proposal rows",
      instruction: "NARROWED 2026-09-14 AFTER THE OPERATOR ANSWERED. He supplied the retention SHAPES for every row and one figure (support communications, a twelve-month ceiling), and he ruled the category correction this step asked for -- artifacts and receipts are separate categories, now performed. What remains HIS is three specific figures and one confirmation, and nothing else on this step: the default expiry behind 'aggressive' for job logs (log_default_period_decision); how long build artifacts stay fetchable (artifact_retention_decision); the tier schedule for abuse and sanctions records (abuse_backoff_schedule_decision); and, through operator_ratification_pass, whether each category is in scope at all and whether its row reads correctly. WHAT IS NO LONGER HIS, so that reading retention_open_question does not send him at questions he cannot answer: the content-addressed cache period is a fabric and materialization eviction policy, and the metering and account-identity periods are counsel's through counsel_retention_period_review. LEAVE EVERY ROW ProposedAwaitingRatification -- do not write RatifiedByOperator anywhere. Ratification is gated by category_settlement_is_coherent on ALL open questions including the per-basis lawful-basis ones, which only counsel can close and which zero spend leaves open, so no row is ratifiable today and flipping one would falsify map_settlement_is_coherent.",
    ),
  },
  start_requirements: no_requirements,
  produces_receipt: "m2-data-map-operator-inputs-supplied",
  progress: StepOpen,
}

